INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','easy','mcq','The glomerular filtration barrier consists of three layers. Which layer is primarily responsible for the charge-selective barrier due to its negative charge?','["A. Glomerular basement membrane","B. Fenestrated endothelium","C. Podocyte foot processes","D. Mesangial cells"]','A','The GBM contains heparan sulphate proteoglycans giving it a strong negative charge, repelling negatively charged proteins like albumin. The fenestrated endothelium prevents cells from passing but has large pores. Podocyte slit diaphragms provide size selectivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','easy','mcq','Juxtamedullary nephrons differ from cortical nephrons primarily by having which feature?','["A. Shorter loops of Henle that remain in the cortex","B. Long loops of Henle that penetrate deep into the medulla","C. Larger Bowman''s capsules","D. Absent juxtaglomerular apparatus"]','B','Juxtamedullary nephrons (~15% of nephrons) have long loops of Henle that descend deep into the inner medulla, essential for creating the medullary osmotic gradient needed for urine concentration. Cortical nephrons have short loops that barely enter the outer medulla.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','medium','mcq','The juxtaglomerular apparatus (JGA) is composed of which three components?','["A. Podocytes, mesangial cells, and parietal epithelium","B. Afferent arteriole, efferent arteriole, and Bowman''s capsule","C. Macula densa, juxtaglomerular cells, and extraglomerular mesangial cells","D. Proximal tubule, distal tubule, and collecting duct"]','C','The JGA comprises the macula densa (specialised dense distal tubule cells sensing NaCl), juxtaglomerular (granular) cells in the afferent arteriole wall that secrete renin, and extraglomerular mesangial (lacis) cells. Together they regulate GFR and renin release via tubuloglomerular feedback.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','medium','mcq','Which structure forms the filtration slits between podocyte foot processes and contains nephrin as a key structural protein?','["A. Glomerular basement membrane","B. Fenestrated capillary endothelium","C. Bowman''s parietal epithelium","D. Slit diaphragm"]','D','The slit diaphragm bridges adjacent podocyte foot processes and is the final filtration barrier. Nephrin is the key protein of the slit diaphragm; mutations in nephrin gene (NPHS1) cause congenital nephrotic syndrome. The slit diaphragm provides both size and charge selectivity.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','medium','mcq','The proximal convoluted tubule is lined by cells with extensive brush borders. What is the primary function of these microvilli?','["A. To dramatically increase the luminal surface area for reabsorption","B. To secrete H+ ions into the tubular lumen","C. To produce erythropoietin","D. To sense tubular flow rate"]','A','The brush border of the PCT increases the luminal surface area approximately 20-fold, facilitating the reabsorption of ~65% of filtered sodium, water, glucose, amino acids, and bicarbonate. Each PCT cell has about 6,500 microvilli on its apical surface.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','medium','mcq','The vasa recta are specialised blood vessels that run parallel to the loops of Henle. What is their primary function?','["A. To deliver oxygen and nutrients to the cortex only","B. To preserve the medullary osmotic gradient via countercurrent exchange","C. To secrete aldosterone into the tubular lumen","D. To filter blood at high pressure"]','B','The vasa recta form hairpin loops alongside the loops of Henle and act as countercurrent exchangers. As blood descends, it equilibrates with the increasingly hyperosmotic medullary interstitium; as it ascends, solutes re-enter the interstitium. This prevents washout of the medullary gradient essential for urine concentration.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','medium','mcq','Mesangial cells within the glomerulus serve multiple functions. Which of the following is NOT a recognised function of mesangial cells?','["A. Phagocytosis of trapped macromolecules","B. Structural support for glomerular capillaries","C. Secretion of renin in response to low perfusion pressure","D. Regulation of glomerular capillary surface area via contraction"]','C','Renin is secreted by juxtaglomerular (granular) cells in the afferent arteriole wall, not by mesangial cells. Mesangial cells provide structural scaffolding, phagocytose immune complexes and macromolecules, contract in response to angiotensin II (reducing filtration surface area), and proliferate in glomerular disease.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','medium','mcq','The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle is impermeable to water. Which cell type characterises this segment?','["A. Principal cells with aquaporin-2 channels","B. Alpha-intercalated cells with H+-ATPase","C. Podocytes with slit diaphragms","D. Tall columnar cells with abundant mitochondria and the Na-K-2Cl cotransporter"]','D','The thick ascending limb (TAL) is lined by tall columnar cells rich in mitochondria to power Na-K-ATPase. The apical Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2, target of loop diuretics) actively reabsorbs NaCl without water, diluting the tubular fluid and concentrating the medullary interstitium. Water impermeability is intrinsic to these cells.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','hard','mcq','A renal biopsy from a patient with nephrotic syndrome shows effacement (flattening) of podocyte foot processes on electron microscopy. This finding most directly impairs which specific filtration barrier function?','["A. Size-selective filtration at the slit diaphragm, allowing large proteins to cross","B. Charge-selective barrier provided by the glomerular basement membrane heparan sulphate","C. Prevention of red blood cell filtration through endothelial fenestrations","D. Renin secretion from juxtaglomerular cells"]','A','Foot process effacement disrupts the slit diaphragm architecture (including nephrin and podocin). The slit diaphragm is the primary size-selective barrier preventing large proteins (>70 kDa) such as albumin from crossing. Its disruption causes massive proteinuria characteristic of nephrotic syndrome. The GBM charge barrier is separate and remains partially intact.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Anatomy','hard','mcq','In the medullary thick ascending limb, the single effect of the countercurrent multiplier is generated by active NaCl reabsorption. What is the approximate osmotic gradient established at any single level between tubular fluid and medullary interstitium?','["A. 50 mOsm/kg","B. 200 mOsm/kg","C. 400 mOsm/kg","D. 600 mOsm/kg"]','B','The "single effect" refers to the ~200 mOsm/kg gradient established at any given horizontal level between the ascending limb (which actively pumps NaCl) and the adjacent descending limb/interstitium. This modest gradient is multiplied longitudinally by the countercurrent flow to produce a total corticomedullary gradient of up to 1200 mOsm/kg at the papilla.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','easy','mcq','Normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in an adult male is approximately:','["A. 125 mL/min","B. 60 mL/min","C. 200 mL/min","D. 50 mL/min"]','A','Normal GFR in an adult male is approximately 125 mL/min (about 180 L/day). In females it is slightly lower (~110 mL/min). GFR is the gold-standard measure of renal function; it is estimated clinically using creatinine-based equations (CKD-EPI, MDRD) or measured using inulin clearance.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','easy','mcq','The filtration fraction represents the fraction of renal plasma flow that is filtered. Its normal value is approximately:','["A. 10%","B. 20%","C. 40%","D. 5%"]','B','Filtration fraction = GFR / RPF = 125/625 = 20%. This means 20% of the plasma entering the glomerulus is filtered. The remaining 80% passes into the efferent arteriole and peritubular capillaries, carrying a higher oncotic pressure that drives tubular reabsorption.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','medium','mcq','Which Starling force opposes glomerular filtration?','["A. Glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure","B. Bowman''s capsule oncotic pressure","C. Glomerular capillary oncotic pressure","D. Net filtration pressure"]','C','The four Starling forces governing GFR are: (1) glomerular capillary hydrostatic pressure (~60 mmHg, favours filtration); (2) Bowman''s capsule hydrostatic pressure (~18 mmHg, opposes); (3) glomerular capillary oncotic pressure (~32 mmHg, opposes filtration); (4) Bowman''s capsule oncotic pressure (~0 mmHg, favours). Net filtration pressure is approximately 60 minus 18 minus 32 = +10 mmHg.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','medium','mcq','Constriction of the efferent arteriole, while holding afferent arteriole tone constant, will produce which effect on GFR and renal blood flow?','["A. Decrease GFR and decrease renal blood flow","B. Increase GFR and increase renal blood flow","C. Decrease GFR and increase renal blood flow","D. Increase GFR and decrease renal blood flow"]','D','Efferent arteriole constriction increases resistance downstream of the glomerulus, raising glomerular hydrostatic pressure and thus GFR. However, it simultaneously reduces total renal blood flow. Angiotensin II preferentially constricts the efferent arteriole, maintaining GFR when renal perfusion pressure falls.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','medium','mcq','The myogenic mechanism of renal autoregulation responds to changes in which stimulus?','["A. Arterial wall stretch caused by changes in perfusion pressure","B. NaCl concentration at the macula densa","C. Angiotensin II levels in the systemic circulation","D. Oxygen tension in the renal cortex"]','A','The myogenic mechanism is intrinsic to the afferent arteriole smooth muscle. When perfusion pressure rises, the vessel wall stretches, triggering vasoconstriction that limits the rise in GFR. Conversely, a fall in pressure causes vasodilation to maintain GFR. This response is independent of tubular function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','medium','mcq','Tubuloglomerular feedback (TGF) is mediated by which mechanism at the macula densa?','["A. Macula densa cells secrete angiotensin II when NaCl rises","B. Increased NaCl delivery triggers ATP/adenosine release causing afferent arteriole constriction","C. High flow stimulates renin release from juxtaglomerular cells","D. Macula densa cells swell and physically compress the afferent arteriole"]','B','When GFR increases, more NaCl reaches the macula densa. The macula densa cells sense this via NKCC2 and release ATP (converted to adenosine) and possibly thromboxane, causing afferent arteriole vasoconstriction. This reduces GFR back toward normal — a negative feedback loop completing the TGF mechanism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','medium','mcq','A patient with severe hypoalbuminaemia (albumin 1.5 g/dL) would be expected to have which of the following changes in GFR?','["A. Markedly decreased GFR due to reduced oncotic pressure in Bowman''s capsule","B. Unchanged GFR because autoregulation compensates completely","C. Increased GFR due to reduced glomerular capillary oncotic pressure opposing filtration","D. Decreased GFR due to tubuloglomerular feedback activation"]','C','Plasma oncotic pressure (normally ~32 mmHg) opposes filtration. In hypoalbuminaemia, oncotic pressure falls, reducing the force opposing filtration and transiently increasing net filtration pressure and GFR. However, in severe cases oedema and compensatory mechanisms partially offset this. Clinically, nephrotic patients often maintain near-normal GFR early.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','medium','mcq','The filtration coefficient (Kf) is the product of which two factors?','["A. GFR and plasma oncotic pressure","B. Net filtration pressure and renal plasma flow","C. Glomerular capillary surface area and hydraulic conductivity of the capillary wall","D. Hydraulic conductivity and filtration surface area of the glomerular capillary"]','D','Kf = hydraulic conductivity (Lp) multiplied by filtration surface area (S). GFR = Kf x net filtration pressure. Kf can be reduced by mesangial cell contraction (reducing S) or glomerular disease damaging the filtration barrier (reducing Lp). Normal glomerular Kf is about 12.5 mL/min/mmHg, much higher than other capillary beds.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','hard','mcq','Renal autoregulation maintains GFR over a mean arterial pressure range of approximately:','["A. 75-160 mmHg","B. 40-80 mmHg","C. 60-120 mmHg","D. 100-200 mmHg"]','A','Renal autoregulation (via myogenic mechanism and TGF) is effective over a MAP range of approximately 75 to 160 mmHg. Below 75 mmHg, afferent arteriolar dilation is maximal and GFR falls with further pressure drops. Above 160 mmHg, constriction is overwhelmed and hyperfiltration occurs. This protects the glomerulus from barotrauma.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','GFR & Filtration','hard','mcq','In a patient taking an ACE inhibitor, why can acute kidney injury occur when the patient becomes volume-depleted?','["A. ACE inhibitors block aldosterone, causing sodium wasting and reducing GFR","B. ACE inhibitors prevent angiotensin II-mediated efferent arteriole constriction; volume depletion then causes glomerular pressure to fall, markedly reducing GFR","C. ACE inhibitors cause direct tubular toxicity when plasma volume falls","D. ACE inhibitors increase prostaglandin synthesis, causing afferent arteriole constriction"]','B','In states of low perfusion (volume depletion, renal artery stenosis, heart failure), the kidney relies on angiotensin II to constrict the efferent arteriole, maintaining glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR. ACE inhibitors block angiotensin II production, preventing this compensatory efferent constriction. Without it, glomerular pressure falls precipitously, causing acute kidney injury.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','easy','mcq','Approximately what percentage of filtered sodium and water is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule?','["A. 65%","B. 25%","C. 10%","D. 90%"]','A','The PCT reabsorbs approximately 65% of filtered sodium, chloride, water, bicarbonate, glucose, and amino acids. Reabsorption is isosmotic — water follows sodium via osmosis, so tubular fluid osmolality remains ~300 mOsm/kg throughout the PCT. This bulk reabsorption reduces the volume delivered to the loop of Henle.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','easy','mcq','Glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule occurs via sodium-glucose cotransporters. Which transporter is the dominant one in the early PCT?','["A. SGLT1 in the late PCT","B. SGLT2 in the early PCT","C. GLUT2 on the apical membrane","D. SGLT3 throughout the PCT"]','B','SGLT2 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 2) is the high-capacity, low-affinity transporter in the early PCT and is responsible for ~90% of glucose reabsorption. SGLT1, a high-affinity, low-capacity transporter, handles the remaining ~10% in the late PCT. SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) exploit this to promote glycosuria in diabetes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','medium','mcq','How is bicarbonate reabsorbed in the proximal tubule?','["A. Direct reabsorption via bicarbonate channels on the apical membrane","B. Via the Na-HCO3 cotransporter on the apical membrane of PCT cells","C. H+ is secreted into the lumen via Na-H exchanger (NHE3), combining with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3, which is split by brush border carbonic anhydrase IV into CO2 and H2O; CO2 enters cells and is rehydrated by intracellular CA II, then HCO3- exits basolaterally","D. Bicarbonate is directly transported across the tight junctions by the paracellular route"]','C','The PCT reabsorbs ~80% of filtered bicarbonate indirectly. NHE3 secretes H+ into the lumen, which combines with filtered HCO3- to form H2CO3, then CO2 + H2O (catalysed by brush border CA IV). CO2 diffuses into the cell and is rehydrated by cytoplasmic CA II to form H2CO3, then H+ + HCO3-. HCO3- exits basolaterally via NBC1 cotransporter.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','medium','mcq','The Tm (transport maximum) for glucose reabsorption is approximately 375 mg/min in adults. What happens when the filtered load of glucose exceeds Tm?','["A. Glucose is secreted into the tubular lumen","B. Additional glucose transporters are recruited from intracellular stores","C. Glucose is converted to glycogen within tubular cells","D. Glucose appears in the urine (glucosuria) because transporters are saturated"]','D','When plasma glucose exceeds ~180-200 mg/dL (the renal threshold), the filtered load exceeds Tm and glucose transporters become saturated. The excess glucose remains in the tubular lumen and is excreted in urine. The "splay" in the titration curve occurs because not all nephrons have identical Tm values — some reach saturation at lower filtered loads.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','medium','mcq','Organic anion secretion in the proximal tubule involves which two-step mechanism?','["A. Basolateral uptake via OAT (organic anion transporter) driven by alpha-ketoglutarate exchange, followed by apical secretion via MRP2 or OAT4","B. Apical uptake followed by basolateral extrusion via P-glycoprotein","C. Simple diffusion across the apical membrane driven by tubular pH","D. Active reabsorption coupled to Na+ gradient"]','A','Organic anions (e.g., para-aminohippurate, urate, penicillin) are taken up from peritubular blood across the basolateral membrane by OAT1/3 via exchange with alpha-ketoglutarate (tertiary active transport driven by Na-K-ATPase). They are then secreted into the tubular lumen via apical transporters (MRP2, OAT4, URAT1). This pathway also handles many drugs and toxins.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','medium','mcq','A patient presents with glucosuria, phosphaturia, aminoaciduria, and type 2 renal tubular acidosis without hyperglycaemia. Which syndrome does this pattern suggest?','["A. Nephrotic syndrome","B. Fanconi syndrome","C. Bartter syndrome","D. Liddle syndrome"]','B','Fanconi syndrome is a generalised dysfunction of the proximal tubule causing failure of reabsorption of glucose, phosphate, amino acids, bicarbonate, uric acid, and potassium — despite normal plasma levels. Causes include Wilson''s disease, galactosaemia, cystinosis, and tenofovir toxicity. The result is multiple urinary spills of normally reabsorbed substances.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','medium','mcq','Which enzyme on the brush border of PCT cells is critical for bicarbonate reabsorption and is inhibited by acetazolamide?','["A. Na-K-ATPase","B. Phospholipase A2","C. Carbonic anhydrase IV (luminal) and carbonic anhydrase II (cytoplasmic)","D. Glutaminase"]','C','Carbonic anhydrase (CA) is essential for PCT bicarbonate reabsorption. CA IV on the brush border catalyses H2CO3 to CO2 + H2O in the lumen. Cytoplasmic CA II rehydrates CO2 to H2CO3 inside the cell. Acetazolamide inhibits both isoforms, blocking HCO3- reabsorption and causing bicarbonate wasting, metabolic acidosis, and increased urine output (used for altitude sickness and glaucoma).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','medium','mcq','Phosphate reabsorption in the PCT occurs primarily via which transporter and is regulated by which hormone?','["A. SGLT2; insulin","B. NHE3; aldosterone","C. NKCC2; ADH","D. NaPi-IIa cotransporter; parathyroid hormone (PTH) downregulates it"]','D','Phosphate is reabsorbed in the PCT primarily by NaPi-IIa (and NaPi-IIc) sodium-phosphate cotransporters on the apical membrane. PTH, acting via cAMP, causes internalisation and degradation of NaPi-IIa, dramatically reducing phosphate reabsorption and causing phosphaturia. FGF23 has a similar effect. This is the primary mechanism of renal phosphate regulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','hard','mcq','In the proximal tubule, the late PCT preferentially reabsorbs chloride relative to bicarbonate. This creates a transtubular electrical potential difference that drives which process?','["A. Paracellular Na+ reabsorption down an electrical gradient, and paracellular Cl- reabsorption driven by its concentration gradient","B. Transcellular Cl- secretion via CFTR channels","C. Active H+ secretion via H+-ATPase","D. K+ secretion via ROMK channels"]','A','In the late PCT, preferential Cl- reabsorption (over HCO3-) creates high luminal Cl- concentration and a lumen-positive potential. This drives paracellular Cl- reabsorption down its concentration gradient, and the lumen-positive potential drives paracellular Na+ reabsorption. About 30-40% of Na+ reabsorption in the late PCT is paracellular.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Proximal Tubule Function','hard','mcq','Uric acid handling in the proximal tubule involves both reabsorption and secretion. Net renal handling results in excretion of approximately what percentage of filtered uric acid, and which transporter is the main reabsorptive one?','["A. 50% excreted; SGLT2","B. 10% excreted; URAT1 (urate anion transporter 1) on the apical membrane","C. 90% excreted; OAT1","D. 25% excreted; NaPi-IIa"]','B','Uric acid undergoes filtration, ~90% reabsorption, then secretion, then further reabsorption — resulting in net excretion of only ~10% of filtered load. URAT1 (SLC22A12) on the apical membrane is the major reabsorptive transporter; its inhibition by probenecid and new uricosuric agents (lesinurad) increases uric acid excretion. URAT1 loss-of-function causes hypouricaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','easy','mcq','The descending thin limb of the loop of Henle is characterised by which permeability properties?','["A. Highly permeable to water, relatively impermeable to NaCl","B. Impermeable to water, highly permeable to NaCl","C. Equally permeable to water and NaCl","D. Permeable to urea but impermeable to water"]','A','The descending thin limb is lined by flat epithelial cells with abundant aquaporin-1 water channels, making it highly permeable to water. As tubular fluid descends into the increasingly hyperosmotic medullary interstitium, water is drawn out osmotically, concentrating the fluid. It is relatively impermeable to NaCl and urea.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','easy','mcq','The Na-K-2Cl cotransporter (NKCC2) in the thick ascending limb is the target of which diuretic class?','["A. Thiazide diuretics","B. Loop diuretics (e.g., furosemide)","C. Potassium-sparing diuretics","D. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors"]','B','Loop diuretics (furosemide, bumetanide, torsemide) block the NKCC2 cotransporter in the thick ascending limb, preventing NaCl reabsorption. Since the TAL is water-impermeable, this also abolishes the medullary osmotic gradient, preventing urinary concentration even in the presence of ADH. Loop diuretics are the most potent diuretics available.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','medium','mcq','The countercurrent multiplier system in the loop of Henle generates a maximum medullary osmolality at the papillary tip of approximately:','["A. 300 mOsm/kg","B. 600 mOsm/kg","C. 1200 mOsm/kg","D. 2400 mOsm/kg"]','C','The combined action of the countercurrent multiplier (loop of Henle) and countercurrent exchanger (vasa recta), along with urea recycling from the collecting duct, generates a medullary gradient ranging from ~300 mOsm/kg at the corticomedullary junction to ~1200 mOsm/kg at the papillary tip. This gradient is essential for producing maximally concentrated urine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','medium','mcq','Urea recycling contributes to the medullary osmotic gradient. How does urea enter the inner medullary interstitium?','["A. Via UT-A2 transporters in the descending thin limb of juxtamedullary nephrons","B. Via active secretion by cells of the thick ascending limb","C. Via passive diffusion across the proximal tubule","D. Via UT-A1 and UT-A3 urea transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct, stimulated by ADH"]','D','ADH stimulates insertion of UT-A1 and UT-A3 urea transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct (IMCD). Urea, concentrated in the IMCD lumen due to water reabsorption, diffuses into the interstitium via these transporters. Some urea then enters the descending thin limb via UT-A2, recirculates through the loop, and returns to the IMCD — completing urea recycling. This accounts for ~50% of the papillary osmolality.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','medium','mcq','As tubular fluid traverses the descending thin limb of a juxtamedullary nephron, what happens to the osmolality of the fluid?','["A. It increases progressively from ~300 mOsm/kg at the cortex to ~1200 mOsm/kg at the papilla","B. It decreases as NaCl is pumped out","C. It remains constant at 300 mOsm/kg","D. It first increases then decreases as fluid turns the hairpin bend"]','A','As fluid descends the descending thin limb, water is continuously drawn out by osmosis into the hyperosmotic interstitium (since the limb is water-permeable but not NaCl-permeable). The tubular fluid osmolality rises from ~300 mOsm/kg at the top to equilibrate with the interstitium (~1200 mOsm/kg at the papillary tip). This concentrated fluid then enters the ascending limb.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','medium','mcq','Bartter syndrome is a hereditary disorder characterised by hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hyperreninaemia without hypertension. Which transporter is affected in the most common type (type I)?','["A. NCC (Na-Cl cotransporter) in the distal tubule","B. NKCC2 (Na-K-2Cl cotransporter) in the thick ascending limb","C. ENaC (epithelial Na channel) in the collecting duct","D. ROMK (K+ channel) — affected in type II Bartter"]','B','Type I Bartter syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in NKCC2 (SLC12A1), the apical cotransporter of the TAL. Without NKCC2, NaCl reabsorption in the TAL fails, mimicking chronic furosemide use. The result is salt wasting, volume depletion, activation of RAAS, secondary hyperaldosteronism, hypokalaemia, and metabolic alkalosis. Type II involves ROMK.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','medium','mcq','The ascending thin limb of the loop of Henle (in juxtamedullary nephrons) is permeable to NaCl but not to water. What drives the passive NaCl efflux from the ascending thin limb?','["A. Active Na-K-ATPase pumping in the ascending thin limb cells","B. The electrochemical gradient created by ADH-mediated water channels","C. The high NaCl concentration in tubular fluid (concentrated in the descending limb) relative to the slightly less concentrated interstitium at that level","D. Angiotensin II stimulating NaCl secretion"]','C','The ascending thin limb lacks Na-K-ATPase activity and relies on passive NaCl diffusion. The tubular fluid entering the ascending limb has very high NaCl concentration (concentrated during descent). Even though the interstitium is hyperosmotic overall, the NaCl concentration gradient slightly favours passive NaCl efflux from the lumen. This passive reabsorption contributes to medullary gradient maintenance in the inner medulla.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','medium','mcq','The vasa recta preserve the medullary osmotic gradient by acting as countercurrent exchangers. What would happen if the vasa recta were replaced by straight-through capillaries?','["A. GFR would increase due to higher peritubular capillary pressure","B. Aldosterone secretion would increase","C. Sodium reabsorption in the PCT would be enhanced","D. The medullary osmotic gradient would be washed out, abolishing urinary concentration ability"]','D','If blood flowed straight through without the hairpin countercurrent exchange, the hyperosmotic solutes in the medullary interstitium would be rapidly washed away. The vasa recta hairpin configuration allows descending blood to lose water to (and gain solutes from) the interstitium, and ascending blood to return solutes to the interstitium — preserving the gradient. Loss of this exchange abolishes the concentration mechanism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','hard','mcq','During chronic loop diuretic use, the medullary osmotic gradient is significantly reduced. Even when the diuretic is stopped, it takes time for maximal concentrating ability to return. Why?','["A. The medullary gradient must be re-established by repeated cycles of NaCl pumping in the TAL and urea accumulation; this takes hours of continuous countercurrent multiplication to rebuild the gradient from near-isotonic to 1200 mOsm/kg","B. Loop diuretics cause permanent structural damage to TAL cells","C. Renin secretion is suppressed for days after diuretic cessation","D. Aquaporin-2 channels are permanently downregulated by loop diuretics"]','A','Loop diuretics disrupt the countercurrent multiplier by blocking NKCC2, reducing medullary osmolality toward isotonic. After cessation, rebuilding the gradient requires sustained NaCl pumping by the TAL and progressive urea accumulation in the inner medulla — a process requiring multiple hours. Until the gradient is re-established, even maximal ADH cannot produce concentrated urine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Loop of Henle & Countercurrent','hard','mcq','A researcher blocks UT-A1 and UT-A3 urea transporters in the inner medullary collecting duct of an experimental animal while maintaining normal ADH levels. What is the predicted maximum urine osmolality?','["A. 1200 mOsm/kg (unchanged, because NaCl gradient alone is sufficient)","B. Approximately 600 mOsm/kg (reduced, because urea normally contributes ~50% of the papillary osmolality)","C. 300 mOsm/kg (isotonic, because the entire gradient is lost)","D. 100 mOsm/kg (dilute urine, because ADH cannot act without urea transporters)"]','B','Urea recycling via UT-A1/A3 in the IMCD contributes approximately half of the papillary tip osmolality (~600 of 1200 mOsm/kg). Blocking these transporters prevents urea from accumulating in the inner medullary interstitium, halving the maximum achievable gradient. The NaCl-generated gradient (from the TAL countercurrent multiplier) persists and can still concentrate urine, but only to about half the normal maximum.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','easy','mcq','In the principal cells of the collecting duct, aldosterone stimulates sodium reabsorption by increasing the expression of which apical channel?','["A. ENaC (epithelial sodium channel)","B. NKCC2 (Na-K-2Cl cotransporter)","C. NCC (Na-Cl cotransporter)","D. SGLT2 (sodium-glucose cotransporter 2)"]','A','Aldosterone binds to mineralocorticoid receptors in principal cells, stimulating transcription of proteins that increase apical ENaC expression and basolateral Na-K-ATPase activity. Na+ enters via ENaC, creating a lumen-negative potential that drives K+ secretion via ROMK. Aldosterone also increases ROMK expression. This is the primary mechanism of aldosterone-mediated sodium retention and potassium excretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','easy','mcq','ADH (vasopressin) increases water permeability of the collecting duct by inserting which water channel protein into the apical membrane?','["A. Aquaporin-1","B. Aquaporin-2","C. Aquaporin-3","D. Aquaporin-4"]','B','ADH binds V2 receptors on principal cells, activating adenylate cyclase then cAMP then PKA, which phosphorylates AQP2 and causes its translocation from intracellular vesicles to the apical membrane. AQP2 is the regulated water channel; AQP3 and AQP4 on the basolateral membrane provide constitutive water exit. Without ADH, the collecting duct is water-impermeable, producing dilute urine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','medium','mcq','Gitelman syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in which transporter, and what is the characteristic electrolyte abnormality?','["A. NKCC2; hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis with hypercalciuria","B. ENaC; hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis","C. NCC (Na-Cl cotransporter, SLC12A3); hypokalaemia, metabolic alkalosis, hypomagnesaemia, and hypocalciuria","D. ROMK; hyperkalaemia and metabolic acidosis"]','C','Gitelman syndrome is the most common hereditary salt-wasting tubulopathy, caused by loss-of-function mutations in NCC (the thiazide-sensitive Na-Cl cotransporter in the DCT). Clinical features mirror thiazide use: hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypocalciuria (distinguishing it from Bartter''s hypercalciuria). DCT NaCl wasting activates RAAS, causing secondary hyperaldosteronism.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','medium','mcq','Alpha-intercalated cells of the collecting duct are primarily responsible for which function?','["A. Sodium reabsorption via ENaC","B. Potassium secretion via ROMK","C. Water reabsorption via aquaporin-2 in response to ADH","D. Active secretion of H+ ions via H+-ATPase and H+-K+-ATPase, and basolateral HCO3- exit via AE1"]','D','Alpha-intercalated cells secrete H+ into the tubular lumen via apical H+-ATPase (V-type) and H+-K+-ATPase. Intracellular carbonic anhydrase II generates H+ and HCO3-; the H+ is secreted and HCO3- exits basolaterally via the Cl-/HCO3- exchanger AE1 (anion exchanger 1). These cells are responsible for final urinary acidification and are upregulated in metabolic acidosis.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','medium','mcq','Liddle syndrome is caused by gain-of-function mutations in ENaC and presents with hypertension, hypokalaemia, and metabolic alkalosis. Why is spironolactone ineffective while amiloride is effective for treatment?','["A. Amiloride directly blocks ENaC and is effective; spironolactone blocks aldosterone receptors and is NOT effective since ENaC is constitutively active regardless of aldosterone","B. Spironolactone is more effective because it reduces aldosterone levels","C. Both are equally effective; either can be used interchangeably","D. Neither drug is effective; only loop diuretics work"]','A','In Liddle syndrome, gain-of-function ENaC mutations cause constitutive (aldosterone-independent) sodium reabsorption by preventing ENaC internalisation. Since the activation is aldosterone-independent, spironolactone (which blocks aldosterone receptors) is ineffective. Amiloride and triamterene, which directly block ENaC channels, are the treatments of choice.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','medium','mcq','Beta-intercalated cells of the collecting duct secrete bicarbonate into the lumen. Under which clinical condition would these cells be most active?','["A. Diabetic ketoacidosis","B. Metabolic alkalosis (e.g., from prolonged vomiting)","C. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease with CO2 retention","D. Diarrhoea-induced metabolic acidosis"]','B','Beta-intercalated cells have the H+-ATPase on the basolateral membrane (secreting H+ into blood) and a Cl-/HCO3- exchanger (pendrin) on the apical membrane (secreting HCO3- into the lumen). They are activated in metabolic alkalosis to excrete excess bicarbonate. In metabolic acidosis, alpha-intercalated cells predominate; the ratio of alpha to beta cells shifts depending on acid-base status.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','medium','mcq','In nephrogenic diabetes insipidus (DI), which of the following mechanisms best explains the polyuria?','["A. Insufficient ADH is produced by the posterior pituitary","B. Aquaporin-2 genes are permanently deleted","C. V2 receptors or downstream signalling components are non-functional, so collecting duct cells cannot respond to ADH","D. Aldosterone excess causes competitive inhibition of ADH binding"]','C','Nephrogenic DI occurs when the kidney fails to respond to ADH. Most commonly, V2 receptor mutations (X-linked) or AQP2 mutations (autosomal) prevent ADH signalling. Less commonly, hypercalcaemia, hypokalaemia, or lithium toxicity downregulate AQP2 expression or impair cAMP signalling. ADH levels are elevated (unlike central DI), but the kidney cannot produce concentrated urine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','medium','mcq','The DCT is the primary site of magnesium reabsorption, mediated by TRPM6 channels. Which stimulus upregulates TRPM6 expression?','["A. Aldosterone","B. Parathyroid hormone via cAMP","C. ADH via V2 receptors","D. Epidermal growth factor (EGF) and insulin"]','D','TRPM6 (transient receptor potential melastatin 6) is the primary apical Mg2+ channel in DCT cells. EGF and insulin upregulate its expression; EGF receptor antibodies (cetuximab, used in colorectal cancer) downregulate TRPM6, causing severe hypomagnesaemia. Proton pump inhibitors also reduce Mg2+ absorption (mainly gut). PTH has a minor role in Mg2+ reabsorption but EGF is the primary regulator of TRPM6.','universal',false,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','hard','mcq','A patient with chronic metabolic acidosis is found to have hypertrophied alpha-intercalated cells and atrophied beta-intercalated cells on renal biopsy. What adaptation explains the maximal urinary acidification observed?','["A. Chronic acidosis upregulates H+-ATPase and H+-K+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells, increases AE1 expression, and promotes conversion of beta-intercalated cells to alpha-intercalated cells; the net result is maximal H+ secretion and minimum HCO3- secretion","B. Aldosterone directly converts beta- to alpha-intercalated cells via mineralocorticoid receptor activation","C. ADH upregulates H+-ATPase expression in principal cells, which begin to secrete H+","D. The thick ascending limb upregulates NHE3 to compensate, and the collecting duct merely fine-tunes this"]','A','In chronic metabolic acidosis, alpha-intercalated cells hypertrophy and increase expression of H+-ATPase (V0/V1 subunits), H+-K+-ATPase, and AE1. Simultaneously, beta-intercalated cells decrease in number and activity. Some data suggest interconversion between subtypes via trafficking of pumps and exchangers. The transcription factor Foxi1 is critical for intercalated cell differentiation. The net effect is maximal net acid excretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Distal Tubule & Collecting Duct','hard','mcq','The water permeability of the inner medullary collecting duct in the absence of ADH is nearly zero. When maximal ADH is present and AQP2 is inserted, what limits the maximum osmolality achievable in the final urine?','["A. The Tm for urea transport in the IMCD","B. The maximum medullary interstitial osmolality achievable by the countercurrent multiplication system (~1200 mOsm/kg in humans), since final urine osmolality can only equilibrate with the interstitium","C. The number of V2 receptors on principal cells","D. The permeability of the peritubular capillaries to water"]','B','Even with maximal AQP2 insertion (maximal ADH), the final urine osmolality cannot exceed the interstitial osmolality at the papillary tip, which is ~1200 mOsm/kg in normally hydrated humans (can reach ~1400 in dehydration). The osmotic equilibration between the tubular fluid and the interstitium is the physical limit. The countercurrent multiplier and urea recycling mechanisms determine this ceiling.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','easy','mcq','What percentage of filtered bicarbonate is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule?','["A. 80%","B. 50%","C. 95%","D. 20%"]','A','The PCT reabsorbs approximately 80% of the filtered bicarbonate load (roughly 4,320 of 4,500 mEq/day). This occurs via H+ secretion by NHE3, with luminal carbonic anhydrase IV and intracellular carbonic anhydrase II mediating the indirect reabsorption of HCO3-. The remaining 20% is reabsorbed in the thick ascending limb and collecting duct.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','easy','mcq','Which buffer system is responsible for most titratable acid excretion in the urine?','["A. Bicarbonate/carbonic acid","B. Monohydrogen/dihydrogen phosphate (HPO4²-/H2PO4-)","C. Ammonia/ammonium","D. Citrate/citric acid"]','B','Titratable acid excretion is mainly due to phosphate buffering. Filtered HPO4²- (pKa 6.8) accepts secreted H+ to become H2PO4-, which is excreted in the urine. On a normal diet, ~30-40 mEq/day of titratable acid is excreted this way. While ammonium excretion is quantitatively larger (up to ~300 mEq/day in acidosis), it is not measured as ''titratable acid'' by the back-titration method.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','medium','mcq','In the proximal tubule, glutamine is metabolised to produce ammonium ions. What happens to these NH4+ ions in the thick ascending limb?','["A. They are excreted directly in the tubular fluid","B. They are converted back to glutamine and returned to the liver","C. NH4+ substitutes for K+ on the NKCC2 cotransporter and is reabsorbed into the medullary interstitium, creating a high interstitial NH3/NH4+ concentration","D. They are secreted into peritubular capillaries via active transport"]','C','In the TAL, NH4+ (which has similar ionic radius to K+) substitutes for K+ on the NKCC2 cotransporter and is reabsorbed into the medullary interstitium. This creates a high NH3/NH4+ concentration in the inner medullary interstitium. NH3 (lipid-soluble) then diffuses into the collecting duct lumen, where it is trapped as NH4+ (by combining with secreted H+), facilitating urinary ammonium excretion. This ''ammonia recycling'' amplifies net acid excretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','medium','mcq','Type 1 (distal) renal tubular acidosis is caused by failure of which specific mechanism?','["A. Carbonic anhydrase II deficiency reducing PCT bicarbonate reabsorption","B. NCC dysfunction in the distal tubule causing salt wasting","C. Aldosterone deficiency reducing Na+ reabsorption and K+ secretion","D. Failure of H+-ATPase in alpha-intercalated cells to secrete H+ in the collecting duct, resulting in urine pH > 5.5 despite systemic acidosis"]','D','Type 1 dRTA results from an inability to acidify urine below pH 5.5 despite systemic metabolic acidosis. The defect is in the H+-ATPase (or less commonly AE1) of alpha-intercalated cells. Features include hyperchloraemic non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis, hypokalaemia (due to RAAS activation), nephrocalcinosis/nephrolithiasis (alkaline urine promotes calcium phosphate stone formation), and osteomalacia (bone acts as buffer).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','medium','mcq','In metabolic acidosis, the kidney increases net acid excretion within hours, but maximal adaptation takes days. What is the principal adaptive mechanism over days?','["A. Upregulation of glutamine uptake and ammoniagenesis in proximal tubule cells, dramatically increasing NH4+ excretion from ~40 mEq/day to over 200-300 mEq/day","B. Increased GFR to filter more bicarbonate","C. Increased aldosterone production stimulating H+ secretion","D. Upregulation of NKCC2 in the thick ascending limb"]','A','The immediate response to acidosis is increased H+ secretion and HCO3- reabsorption. Over 3-5 days, proximal tubule cells markedly upregulate glutaminase and phosphate-dependent glutaminase, dramatically increasing ammoniagenesis. NH4+ excretion can rise from ~40 mEq/day to >300 mEq/day in severe acidosis. This ''ammonium adaptation'' is the principal mechanism of sustained urinary acid excretion and is coordinated by acidosis-induced gene expression changes.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','medium','mcq','Type 2 (proximal) renal tubular acidosis involves a defect in proximal tubule bicarbonate reabsorption. Which feature distinguishes it from type 1 dRTA?','["A. Patients with type 2 have a urine pH that cannot drop below 5.5","B. In type 2, once plasma HCO3- falls below the lowered threshold (~15-18 mEq/L), the distal nephron CAN acidify urine (pH < 5.5); presenting features include hypokalaemia, phosphaturia, glucosuria (if Fanconi), and no nephrocalcinosis","C. Type 2 causes hyperkalaemia while type 1 causes hypokalaemia","D. Type 2 is treated with sodium bicarbonate whereas type 1 is untreatable"]','B','In type 2 pRTA, the PCT threshold for HCO3- reabsorption is lowered. When plasma HCO3- is normal, it is spilled in the urine and urine pH is high. But once plasma HCO3- falls below the new threshold, the distal nephron reabsorbs all delivered HCO3- and can acidify urine normally (pH <5.5). This distinguishes it from type 1 where the distal acidification is always impaired. No nephrocalcinosis occurs in type 2.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','medium','mcq','In chronic kidney disease, the inability to excrete the daily acid load results in a characteristic pattern. Which finding best describes the acid-base disturbance?','["A. High anion gap metabolic alkalosis","B. Normal anion gap metabolic alkalosis","C. High anion gap metabolic acidosis due to retained organic acids (sulphates, phosphates) and reduced capacity to excrete NH4+","D. Respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation"]','C','In advanced CKD (GFR <20-30 mL/min), there is insufficient functioning nephron mass to excrete the daily acid load (~70 mEq H+). Two mechanisms contribute: (1) reduced NH4+ excretion as fewer tubular cells metabolise glutamine; (2) accumulation of unmeasured anions (sulphate, phosphate, hippurate, organic acids) from incomplete metabolism, raising the anion gap. This produces high anion gap metabolic acidosis (uremic acidosis).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','medium','mcq','Type 4 renal tubular acidosis (hyperkalaemic RTA) is most commonly due to:','["A. Loss of function of H+-ATPase in the collecting duct","B. Proximal tubule carbonic anhydrase deficiency","C. Overactivity of aldosterone receptors","D. Hypoaldosteronism (or aldosterone resistance), leading to impaired K+ excretion and NH4+ synthesis/excretion due to hyperkalaemia-induced intracellular alkalosis suppressing renal ammoniagenesis"]','D','Type 4 RTA results from hypoaldosteronism (e.g., Addison''s disease, hyporeninism in diabetic nephropathy, ACE inhibitors) or aldosterone resistance. Without aldosterone, both K+ secretion and H+ secretion in the collecting duct are impaired. The resultant hyperkalaemia directly suppresses ammoniagenesis in PCT cells (intracellular alkalosis from K+ entering cells), dramatically reducing NH4+ excretion. The result is hyperchloraemic non-anion-gap metabolic acidosis with hyperkalaemia.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','hard','mcq','In the collecting duct, NH3 diffusion trapping allows large amounts of NH4+ to be excreted. What determines the direction and magnitude of NH3 diffusion?','["A. The pH gradient between the medullary interstitium (high NH3 due to recycling) and the collecting duct lumen (low NH3 because H+ secretion converts it to NH4+); steep gradients produced by very low lumen pH maximise NH3 trapping and NH4+ excretion","B. Active transport of NH4+ via the Na-K-ATPase","C. Aquaporin-mediated transport of NH3 across the collecting duct","D. The systemic arterial pH determines net NH3 flux"]','A','NH3 (pKa 9.2) is lipid-soluble and crosses membranes freely by diffusion. The medullary interstitium has high total NH3+NH4+ concentration (built up by TAL NKCC2 reabsorption of NH4+). When collecting duct lumen pH is low (due to H+-ATPase activity), nearly all NH3 in the lumen is converted to NH4+ (trapped, as NH4+ is membrane-impermeant). This maintains a low luminal NH3 concentration, sustaining the diffusion gradient from interstitium to lumen. More acidic urine = more NH3 trapping = more NH4+ excretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Acid-Base Regulation','hard','mcq','The urinary anion gap (UAG = [Na+]u + [K+]u - [Cl-]u) is used to estimate urine NH4+ excretion. In a patient with diarrhoea-induced normal anion gap metabolic acidosis and an appropriate renal response, what is the expected UAG?','["A. Positive (>0), indicating adequate NH4+ excretion","B. Negative (<0), indicating high unmeasured cation (NH4+) excretion in the urine as a marker of appropriate renal acid excretion","C. Zero, indicating no net acid excretion","D. Strongly positive (>30 mEq/L), indicating type 1 dRTA"]','B','NH4+ is a positive cation not measured by routine urine electrolytes. A negative UAG means Cl- exceeds Na++K+, implying a large unmeasured cation -- most commonly NH4+ in the setting of metabolic acidosis. A negative UAG (e.g., -20 to -50 mEq/L) indicates the kidney is appropriately increasing NH4+ excretion, as expected in GI bicarbonate losses (diarrhoea). A positive UAG in metabolic acidosis suggests the kidney is NOT excreting NH4+ normally, pointing to RTA.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','easy','mcq','Approximately what fraction of filtered potassium is reabsorbed in the proximal convoluted tubule?','["A. 65%","B. 25%","C. 90%","D. 10%"]','A','The PCT reabsorbs approximately 65% of filtered K+ passively, following Na+ and water reabsorption. Since PCT reabsorption is isosmotic and K+ moves paracellularly driven by solvent drag and the lumen-positive potential in the late PCT, it is linked to overall sodium and water reabsorption. The thick ascending limb reabsorbs an additional 25-30%, leaving ~10% to enter the distal nephron for regulated secretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','easy','mcq','The ROMK channel (Kir1.1) in the principal cells of the collecting duct is responsible for:','["A. Potassium reabsorption from the tubular lumen","B. Potassium secretion from the cell into the tubular lumen","C. Sodium entry into the cell from the lumen","D. Chloride secretion into the lumen"]','B','ROMK (renal outer medullary K+ channel, Kir1.1) is the primary apical K+ channel in principal cells mediating K+ secretion. K+ enters the cell via basolateral Na-K-ATPase (creating high intracellular K+) and exits apically down its electrochemical gradient via ROMK into the tubular lumen. Aldosterone upregulates both Na-K-ATPase and ROMK, increasing K+ secretion. Amiloride blockade of ENaC reduces the driving force for ROMK-mediated K+ secretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','medium','mcq','A patient develops hypokalaemia after starting a new medication. Which drug class most likely causes hypokalaemia through increased aldosterone-mediated potassium secretion?','["A. ACE inhibitors","B. Beta-blockers","C. Loop diuretics","D. Potassium-sparing diuretics"]','C','Loop diuretics (furosemide) cause hypokalaemia by two mechanisms: (1) increased distal tubular flow rate (more tubular fluid flushes K+ away from principal cell ROMK, maintaining the K+ concentration gradient for secretion); (2) secondary hyperaldosteronism from volume depletion (aldosterone upregulates ENaC and ROMK). ACE inhibitors and K+-sparing diuretics cause hyperkalaemia. Beta-blockers cause mild hyperkalaemia by impairing K+ cellular uptake.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','medium','mcq','In metabolic acidosis, hyperkalaemia commonly occurs. What is the primary mechanism of this transcellular K+ shift?','["A. Acidosis stimulates ROMK channels to close, retaining K+ in the body","B. Acidosis inhibits aldosterone secretion, reducing K+ excretion","C. Acidosis directly damages proximal tubule cells, impairing K+ reabsorption","D. H+ ions move into cells to be buffered intracellularly; in exchange, K+ exits cells to maintain electroneutrality, raising plasma K+ (approximately 0.6 mEq/L rise per 0.1 unit fall in pH for mineral acids)"]','D','In metabolic acidosis from mineral acids (HCl, H2SO4), H+ ions enter cells and are buffered by intracellular proteins and bicarbonate. To maintain electrical neutrality, K+ exits cells in exchange, raising plasma K+. The rule of thumb: plasma K+ rises ~0.6 mEq/L per 0.1 pH unit decrease. This does not apply to organic acidoses (lactic acid, ketoacids) where anion entry into cells allows electroneutral H+ entry without K+ shift.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','medium','mcq','High dietary potassium intake increases K+ secretion in the collecting duct via which mechanism(s)?','["A. Both direct stimulation of principal cell Na-K-ATPase by high plasma K+ and stimulation of aldosterone secretion from the adrenal zona glomerulosa by high plasma K+","B. Suppression of ADH, causing increased tubular flow that washes K+ away","C. High dietary K+ inhibits ROMK to prevent K+ loss","D. High dietary K+ activates the macula densa to increase GFR"]','A','High plasma K+ directly stimulates aldosterone secretion from the adrenal cortex (the primary stimulus, independent of angiotensin II) and also directly activates Na-K-ATPase in principal cells. Aldosterone upregulates ENaC (creating lumen negative potential driving K+ secretion) and ROMK (increasing K+ channel number). High K+ intake also increases BKCa (maxi-K) channel activity in the collecting duct in response to increased tubular flow.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','medium','mcq','Trimethoprim (an antibiotic) commonly causes hyperkalaemia. What is the mechanism?','["A. Trimethoprim inhibits aldosterone synthesis in the adrenal cortex","B. Trimethoprim blocks ENaC in the collecting duct (like amiloride), reducing the lumen-negative potential and thereby decreasing K+ secretion","C. Trimethoprim inhibits ROMK directly","D. Trimethoprim causes type 4 RTA by blocking H+-ATPase"]','B','Trimethoprim has structural similarity to amiloride and blocks epithelial sodium channels (ENaC) in the collecting duct. This reduces Na+ reabsorption and the lumen-negative transepithelial potential that drives K+ secretion via ROMK. The result is hyperkalaemia and mild metabolic acidosis (type 4-like RTA). The effect is dose-dependent and more pronounced in patients with reduced renal function or those taking other K+-sparing agents.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','medium','mcq','In the presence of high aldosterone levels (primary hyperaldosteronism), the expected electrolyte abnormalities include:','["A. Hyperkalaemia, hypernatraemia, and metabolic acidosis","B. Hypokalaemia, hyponatraemia, and metabolic acidosis","C. Hypokalaemia, mild hypernatraemia, and metabolic alkalosis","D. Hyperkalaemia, hyponatraemia, and metabolic alkalosis"]','C','Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn''s syndrome) causes: (1) Na+ retention -> volume expansion -> mild hypernatraemia and hypertension; (2) K+ secretion -> hypokalaemia; (3) H+ secretion by alpha-intercalated cells (stimulated by aldosterone) -> metabolic alkalosis; (4) despite volume expansion, aldosterone escape limits oedema. The classic triad is hypertension + hypokalaemia + metabolic alkalosis with suppressed renin.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','medium','mcq','BK (big conductance, maxi-K) channels in the collecting duct contribute to K+ secretion particularly under which conditions?','["A. Low tubular flow and low aldosterone","B. Systemic acidosis with low plasma K+","C. ADH-mediated water reabsorption with concentrated tubular fluid","D. High tubular flow rate (such as during high urine output from loop diuretics) and after a high-K+ meal"]','D','BKCa channels (maxi-K, KCNMA1) are mechanosensitive K+ channels activated by high tubular flow (stretch) and by aldosterone. They are particularly important during states of high urine flow (loop diuretics, high fluid intake) when increased flow prevents luminal K+ from building up, maintaining the driving force for K+ secretion. They complement ROMK to provide the ''flow-stimulated'' component of K+ secretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','hard','mcq','A patient with type 4 RTA and hyperkalaemia has a plasma aldosterone level that is appropriately high. Despite this, the patient fails to excrete K+ normally. This pattern suggests aldosterone resistance. Which condition classically causes this?','["A. Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 (PHA1), caused by mutations in ENaC subunits (autosomal recessive form) or mineralocorticoid receptors (autosomal dominant), causing salt wasting, hyperkalaemia, and high aldosterone levels because the collecting duct cannot respond to aldosterone","B. Gitelman syndrome, where NCC loss causes hypokalemia","C. Liddle syndrome, where ENaC gain-of-function causes hypertension","D. Bartter syndrome type 1, where NKCC2 loss causes hypokalaemia"]','A','Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 (PHA1) mimics aldosterone deficiency despite elevated aldosterone levels. The autosomal recessive form involves loss-of-function ENaC mutations (SCNN1A, B, or G); the autosomal dominant form involves mineralocorticoid receptor mutations. Either way, the collecting duct cannot respond to aldosterone, producing severe salt wasting, hyperkalaemia, and metabolic acidosis in neonates. High aldosterone fails to stimulate ENaC or ROMK, so K+ secretion is impaired.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Potassium Handling','hard','mcq','During exercise, large amounts of K+ are released from contracting skeletal muscle. What renal mechanism rapidly increases K+ excretion when plasma K+ rises?','["A. Immediate upregulation of ROMK gene transcription in principal cells takes 24 hours","B. The direct kaliuretic effect of elevated plasma K+: high K+ stimulates aldosterone secretion within minutes-to-hours (aldosterone surge), directly activates Na-K-ATPase and ROMK in principal cells, and increases the electrochemical driving force for K+ secretion; this is supplemented by exercise-induced increases in aldosterone from adrenal stimulation","C. GFR increases dramatically during exercise, increasing filtered K+ load","D. Metabolic acidosis during exercise causes K+ to shift back into cells immediately"]','B','The kidney''s response to acute hyperkalaemia involves multiple rapid mechanisms: (1) elevated plasma K+ directly stimulates aldosterone secretion from the adrenal zona glomerulosa within minutes; (2) high plasma K+ directly activates basolateral Na-K-ATPase in principal cells (increasing intracellular K+ and driving force for secretion); (3) increased K+ concentration in peritubular blood increases the gradient for ROMK secretion. Together these mechanisms can increase K+ excretion several-fold within hours of K+ loading.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','easy','mcq','Normal renal blood flow constitutes approximately what fraction of cardiac output?','["A. 20-25%","B. 5-10%","C. 40-50%","D. 10-15%"]','A','The kidneys receive approximately 1,100-1,200 mL/min of blood, representing 20-25% of the resting cardiac output (~5 L/min). This is disproportionately high relative to kidney mass (~0.5% body weight), reflecting the high metabolic demands of active transport and the need for continuous high-flow filtration. Renal plasma flow (accounting for haematocrit ~45%) is approximately 600-650 mL/min.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','easy','mcq','Renin is secreted by juxtaglomerular (granular) cells in response to three stimuli. Which of the following is NOT a stimulus for renin release?','["A. Decreased stretch of the afferent arteriole wall (low renal perfusion pressure)","B. Increased NaCl concentration at the macula densa","C. Increased sympathetic nerve activity acting via beta-1 adrenoreceptors on JG cells","D. Decreased NaCl delivery to the macula densa"]','B','Renin is released in response to three signals: (1) decreased afferent arteriole wall stretch (baroreceptor mechanism -- low perfusion pressure); (2) DECREASED NaCl delivery to the macula densa (TGF operates in reverse for renin); (3) increased sympathetic activity via beta-1 receptors on JG cells. INCREASED NaCl at the macula densa inhibits renin release (and causes afferent constriction via TGF), not stimulates it.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II. ACE also degrades which other vasoactive peptide?','["A. Aldosterone","B. Renin","C. Bradykinin","D. Endothelin-1"]','C','ACE (also called kininase II) serves a dual function: it converts angiotensin I (10 AA) to angiotensin II (8 AA) and simultaneously inactivates bradykinin (a potent vasodilator and bronchoconstrictor). ACE inhibitors therefore both reduce angiotensin II production AND increase bradykinin levels. The accumulation of bradykinin is responsible for the dry cough (15-20% of patients) and rare angioedema seen with ACE inhibitors.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Angiotensin II has direct actions on the proximal tubule. Which is the main proximal tubular effect?','["A. Inhibition of NHE3, causing natriuresis","B. Stimulation of aquaporin-2 insertion in the apical membrane","C. Inhibition of Na-K-ATPase, reducing Na+ reabsorption","D. Stimulation of NHE3 (via AT1 receptor and PKC), increasing Na+ and HCO3- reabsorption, and inhibition of NaPi-IIa, reducing phosphate reabsorption"]','D','Angiotensin II acting on AT1 receptors in PCT cells stimulates NHE3 via PKC and PI3K pathways, increasing apical H+ secretion and coupled Na+ reabsorption. This also enhances bicarbonate reabsorption. At high concentrations, Ang II also inhibits NaPi-IIa (phosphate cotransporter). The net effect is increased Na+ and water reabsorption in the PCT, contributing to the anti-natriuretic actions of the RAAS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Renal prostaglandins (particularly PGE2 and PGI2) are produced by the kidney in response to:','["A. Increased sympathetic tone, angiotensin II, and endothelin, acting as a counter-regulatory vasodilatory system that maintains RBF and GFR during states of low renal perfusion","B. Increased renal perfusion pressure to cause vasodilation and increase GFR","C. High sodium intake, increasing renal blood flow","D. ADH, causing vasoconstriction of the afferent arteriole"]','A','Renal prostaglandins (PGE2, PGI2) are synthesised by medullary interstitial cells, mesangial cells, and endothelial cells in response to vasoconstrictor stimuli (sympathetics, angiotensin II, endothelin). They act as local vasodilators, particularly on the afferent arteriole, counterbalancing vasoconstriction and protecting GFR. This is why NSAIDs (which block prostaglandin synthesis) can precipitate acute kidney injury in states of reduced renal perfusion (heart failure, cirrhosis, volume depletion).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Atrial natriuretic peptide (ANP) is released in response to atrial stretch (volume overload). What are its direct renal effects?','["A. ANP constricts the afferent arteriole, reducing GFR and causing natriuresis","B. ANP dilates the afferent arteriole and constricts the efferent arteriole (increasing GFR), inhibits renin and aldosterone secretion, increases sodium excretion by reducing NCC and ENaC activity, and inhibits ADH effects","C. ANP stimulates aldosterone secretion and increases Na+ reabsorption","D. ANP activates RAAS to restore blood pressure"]','B','ANP acts via cGMP to produce a natriuretic, diuretic, and vasodilatory response. In the kidney: afferent dilation + efferent constriction increases GFR; reduced renin secretion decreases angiotensin II and aldosterone; direct inhibition of ENaC in the collecting duct reduces Na+ reabsorption; inhibition of ADH release/action reduces water reabsorption. ANP is an important counterpart to RAAS in volume regulation.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','medium','mcq','In bilateral renal artery stenosis, RAAS is chronically activated. Why is it particularly dangerous to use ACE inhibitors in this condition?','["A. ACE inhibitors directly damage renal arteries","B. ACE inhibitors increase renin secretion, worsening hypertension","C. Both kidneys rely on high angiotensin II to maintain GFR via efferent arteriole constriction; ACE inhibitors abolish this compensatory mechanism bilaterally, causing rapid bilateral GFR loss and acute kidney injury","D. ACE inhibitors cause bilateral renal vein thrombosis"]','C','In bilateral RAS, both kidneys are hypoperfused and depend on angiotensin II to constrict efferent arterioles bilaterally, maintaining glomerular hydrostatic pressure and GFR. ACE inhibitors block angiotensin II production, simultaneously removing the only mechanism sustaining GFR in both kidneys. The result is bilateral GFR collapse and acute kidney injury. This is the classic contraindication for ACE inhibitors and ARBs in bilateral RAS.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','medium','mcq','Which adrenoreceptor subtype on renal afferent and efferent arterioles mediates vasoconstriction in response to increased sympathetic tone?','["A. Beta-2 adrenoreceptors causing vasodilation","B. Beta-1 adrenoreceptors stimulating renin release only","C. Alpha-2 adrenoreceptors causing vasodilation","D. Alpha-1 adrenoreceptors causing vasoconstriction of renal arterioles, reducing RBF and GFR"]','D','Alpha-1 adrenoreceptors on renal vascular smooth muscle mediate vasoconstriction in response to sympathetic stimulation. Strong sympathetic activation (haemorrhage, severe heart failure) causes profound renal vasoconstriction via alpha-1 receptors, dramatically reducing RBF and GFR to preserve central blood pressure. Additionally, beta-1 receptors on JG cells stimulate renin release, activating RAAS for further sodium retention.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','hard','mcq','Endothelin-1 (ET-1), produced by renal endothelial cells, exerts different effects depending on receptor subtype. Via ETA receptors, what is the predominant renal vascular effect, and via ETB receptors on tubular cells, what is the tubular effect?','["A. ETA: vasoconstriction of afferent and efferent arterioles reducing RBF and GFR; ETB on collecting duct: inhibition of ENaC and promotion of Na+ excretion; ETB on inner medullary cells: inhibition of ADH-stimulated water reabsorption","B. ETA: vasodilation increasing GFR; ETB: stimulation of aldosterone","C. ETA: renin release; ETB: ADH stimulation","D. Both ETA and ETB cause identical vasodilation"]','A','ET-1 has complex effects. ETA receptors (mainly vascular smooth muscle) mediate potent vasoconstriction of both afferent and efferent arterioles, reducing RBF and GFR. ETB receptors on the inner medullary collecting duct inhibit ENaC-mediated Na+ reabsorption (natriuretic effect) and counteract ADH-stimulated water reabsorption. ETB receptors on endothelial cells also trigger NO and prostacyclin release (vasodilatory). ET-1 is the most potent endogenous vasoconstrictor and plays a role in AKI pathophysiology.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','RAAS & Renal Blood Flow','hard','mcq','The pressure-natriuresis relationship describes how increases in renal perfusion pressure directly increase sodium and water excretion. What is the primary intrarenal mechanism mediating this pressure-natriuresis response?','["A. High pressure directly damages glomerular endothelial cells, causing massive protein loss and osmotic diuresis","B. Elevated renal perfusion pressure increases interstitial hydrostatic pressure in the renal medulla, reducing peritubular capillary reabsorption from the PCT and loop of Henle, and also reducing Ang II and aldosterone activity; together these reduce tubular Na+ reabsorption leading to natriuresis","C. High pressure activates TGF maximally, reducing GFR","D. High pressure causes baroreceptor-mediated ADH suppression, producing aquaresis"]','B','Pressure-natriuresis involves: (1) increased medullary interstitial hydrostatic pressure reducing the reabsorptive force for peritubular capillaries (Starling forces), thereby reducing PCT and loop Na+/water reabsorption; (2) paracrine release of nitric oxide and prostaglandins reducing tubular Na+ transport; (3) reduced angiotensin II (via stretch-suppressed renin release) decreasing NHE3 activity; (4) reduced aldosterone reducing ENaC activity. This mechanism is central to long-term blood pressure control by renal Na+ excretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','easy','mcq','The clearance of inulin is used as the gold standard to measure GFR because:','["A. Inulin is freely filtered, not reabsorbed, not secreted, and not metabolised by the kidney","B. Inulin is actively secreted by the proximal tubule, so it measures tubular secretion","C. Inulin is partially reabsorbed in the collecting duct","D. Inulin is bound to plasma proteins and is not filtered"]','A','Inulin (a fructose polymer) is the gold standard GFR marker because it fulfils all criteria: freely filtered at the glomerulus (not protein-bound), not reabsorbed, not secreted, not synthesised or metabolised by the kidney. Therefore, the amount filtered equals the amount excreted. GFR = Uinulin x V / Pinulin. Clinically, creatinine clearance is used as an approximation, but slightly overestimates GFR due to tubular secretion of creatinine.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','easy','mcq','Para-aminohippurate (PAH) is used to estimate renal plasma flow (RPF) because at low plasma concentrations:','["A. PAH is filtered only, like inulin","B. PAH is almost completely cleared from plasma in a single pass through the kidney (extraction ratio ~90%), combining filtration and near-complete tubular secretion","C. PAH is reabsorbed in the proximal tubule","D. PAH is synthesised by the kidney itself"]','B','At low plasma PAH concentrations (below the Tm for PAH secretion), ~90% of PAH in renal arterial blood is cleared per pass. This occurs because PAH is both filtered and actively secreted by proximal tubule organic anion transporters (OAT1/3). RPF = UPAH x V / PPAH / extraction ratio. Since extraction ratio is ~0.9, ERPF (effective RPF) = PAH clearance approximately 0.9 x true RPF. ERPF is typically ~600-650 mL/min.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','medium','mcq','A patient has a plasma creatinine of 1.0 mg/dL, urine creatinine of 100 mg/dL, and urine flow rate of 1 mL/min. What is the creatinine clearance?','["A. 10 mL/min","B. 50 mL/min","C. 100 mL/min","D. 1 mL/min"]','C','Clearance = (Urine concentration x Urine flow rate) / Plasma concentration = (100 mg/dL x 1 mL/min) / 1.0 mg/dL = 100 mL/min. Note that units cancel because both concentrations are in mg/dL. Creatinine clearance slightly overestimates true GFR (measured by inulin) by ~10-20% because creatinine is also secreted by the proximal tubule via organic cation transporters. Cimetidine blocks this secretion and is sometimes used to get a truer GFR estimate.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','medium','mcq','The transport maximum (Tm) for glucose reabsorption in the proximal tubule is approximately 375 mg/min. The splay in the titration curve (glucose excretion beginning before the theoretical threshold) occurs because:','["A. Some glucose is secreted at low filtered loads","B. SGLT2 has infinite capacity and never saturates","C. Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors reduce glucose transport","D. Nephrons have heterogeneous Tm values and Km values, so individual nephrons begin spilling glucose at different filtered loads; the average threshold appears lower than the theoretical Tm"]','D','In an ideal kidney, no glucosuria would occur until filtered load exactly exceeds Tm (theoretical threshold). However, individual nephrons differ in their Tm values and their SGLT2 affinity (Km). Some nephrons saturate their transporters at lower filtered loads, causing glucose to appear in urine before the average Tm is reached. This spreads the titration curve into a gradual slope (''splay'') rather than a sharp threshold, with glucosuria beginning around 180 mg/dL plasma glucose (renal threshold) even though Tm corresponds to ~300 mg/dL.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','medium','mcq','The filtered load of substance X is 100 mg/min. Urinary excretion is 20 mg/min. What is the net tubular reabsorption?','["A. 80 mg/min","B. 120 mg/min","C. 20 mg/min","D. 100 mg/min"]','A','Net tubular reabsorption = Filtered load - Excretion rate = 100 - 20 = 80 mg/min. If excretion exceeds filtered load, net secretion is occurring. If filtered load exceeds excretion, net reabsorption is occurring. For substances with both reabsorption and secretion (like uric acid, creatinine), the ''net'' value reflects the algebraic sum; gross reabsorption and gross secretion must be measured separately using specific techniques.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','medium','mcq','Free water clearance (CH2O) is calculated as: CH2O = V minus Cosm. In a patient who is dehydrated and producing concentrated urine (osmolality 800 mOsm/kg, urine flow 0.5 mL/min, plasma osmolality 290 mOsm/kg), calculate CH2O.','["A. +0.5 mL/min","B. -0.88 mL/min","C. +1.0 mL/min","D. 0 mL/min"]','B','Cosm = (Uosm x V) / Posm = (800 x 0.5) / 290 = 400/290 = 1.38 mL/min. CH2O = V - Cosm = 0.5 - 1.38 = -0.88 mL/min. Negative free water clearance (TcH2O or CH2O negative) indicates the kidney is retaining free water (concentrating urine), which is appropriate in dehydration. Positive CH2O occurs when dilute urine is produced (ADH absent), indicating free water excretion.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','medium','mcq','The extraction ratio (ER) of PAH is 0.9 at low plasma concentrations. As plasma PAH concentration rises above the Tm for PAH secretion, what happens to ER?','["A. ER increases toward 1.0 as more PAH is filtered","B. ER stays at 0.9 since filtration continues","C. ER decreases toward the filtered fraction alone (~0.2), because tubular secretion becomes saturated while filtration continues; total clearance approaches filtration fraction x RPF","D. ER increases to 1.0 because peritubular secretion maximises"]','C','At low PAH concentrations, tubular secretion plus filtration gives ER ~0.9. As PAH concentration rises above Tm, the secretory component saturates. Above Tm, incremental increases in plasma PAH are excreted only by filtration (which contributes ER = filtration fraction approximately 0.2). Thus ER progressively decreases from 0.9 toward 0.2. This is why PAH must be infused at low concentrations to measure ERPF accurately.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','medium','mcq','The GFR is 125 mL/min and the renal plasma flow is 625 mL/min. Substance Y has a plasma concentration of 2 mg/dL, a urine concentration of 50 mg/dL, and a urine flow of 1 mL/min. What is the clearance of Y, and is it being reabsorbed, secreted, or neither?','["A. 25 mL/min; net secretion","B. 125 mL/min; neither (same as inulin-like behaviour)","C. 50 mL/min; net reabsorption","D. 25 mL/min; net reabsorption (clearance < GFR indicates net reabsorption)"]','D','Clearance of Y = (50 x 1) / 2 = 25 mL/min. Since GFR is 125 mL/min and clearance of Y is only 25 mL/min, less of Y is being excreted than what was filtered. This means the tubules are net reabsorbing Y. If clearance > GFR (>125 mL/min), net secretion would be occurring. Clearance = GFR would indicate neither reabsorption nor secretion (inulin-like).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','hard','mcq','Cystatin C is increasingly used as an alternative GFR marker. Which property makes it superior to creatinine as a GFR marker in certain populations?','["A. Cystatin C is produced at a constant rate by all nucleated cells and its plasma level is not significantly affected by muscle mass, diet, or ethnicity; it is freely filtered, not secreted, and only slightly reabsorbed (and catabolised) in the PCT; serum cystatin C rises earlier than creatinine when GFR falls","B. Cystatin C is actively secreted by the proximal tubule, making its clearance higher than GFR","C. Cystatin C is not filtered by the glomerulus and measures only tubular function","D. Cystatin C is only useful in patients with normal body weight"]','A','Cystatin C advantages over creatinine: (1) not affected by muscle mass (better in sarcopenic elderly, amputees, cirrhosis); (2) not affected by diet (creatinine rises with high meat intake); (3) minimal racial variation in production rate; (4) rises earlier than creatinine when GFR falls (smaller ''blind spot'' in mild CKD). However, cystatin C is elevated by corticosteroids and thyroid disease (increased production) and can be misleading in these settings.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Renal Clearance & Tubular Transport','hard','mcq','In a patient with a GFR of 10 mL/min (CKD stage 5), the serum creatinine is 8 mg/dL. As GFR falls below ~60 mL/min, tubular secretion of creatinine becomes a larger fraction of total creatinine excretion. What is the clinical implication?','["A. Creatinine clearance becomes a better estimate of GFR at low GFR values","B. At low GFR, creatinine clearance substantially overestimates true GFR because tubular secretion contributes a larger proportion of creatinine excretion; cystatin C-based equations or measured inulin clearance are more accurate in advanced CKD","C. Creatinine clearance equals GFR exactly in CKD due to compensatory decrease in secretion","D. Tubular secretion of creatinine ceases in CKD, making creatinine clearance a pure measure of filtration"]','B','Normally, tubular secretion adds ~10-20% to creatinine excretion (creatinine clearance is ~10-20% above true GFR). In advanced CKD, as GFR falls, secreted creatinine becomes a larger fraction of total creatinine excretion (because proportionally less is filtered). This causes creatinine clearance to overestimate GFR by up to 50-100% in severe CKD. Additionally, gut bacteria increasingly degrade creatinine to creatine in CKD (extrarenal elimination). CKD-EPI equations partially correct for this but have limitations at very low GFR.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','easy','mcq','The normal plasma osmolality is maintained between approximately:','["A. 285-295 mOsm/kg","B. 250-270 mOsm/kg","C. 310-330 mOsm/kg","D. 270-280 mOsm/kg"]','A','Normal plasma osmolality is tightly regulated at 285-295 mOsm/kg (mean ~290 mOsm/kg). Osmolality is primarily determined by sodium and its anions (~2 x [Na+]), glucose, and urea. The formula is: Posm approximately 2[Na+] + glucose/18 + BUN/2.8. Osmoreceptors in the anterior hypothalamus detect deviations as small as 1-2% and respond by adjusting ADH release and thirst, keeping osmolality remarkably constant.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','easy','mcq','ADH (antidiuretic hormone, vasopressin) is synthesised in the hypothalamus and released from which structure?','["A. Anterior pituitary","B. Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis)","C. Adrenal medulla","D. Juxtaglomerular cells of the kidney"]','B','ADH is synthesised by magnocellular neurons in the supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei of the hypothalamus. It is transported down axons and stored in vesicles in the posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis). Released ADH enters the circulation and acts primarily on V2 receptors in collecting duct principal cells to insert AQP2 and on V1 receptors in vascular smooth muscle to cause vasoconstriction.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','medium','mcq','The minimum urine osmolality achievable when ADH is completely absent (maximum diluting capacity) is approximately:','["A. 100 mOsm/kg but not lower","B. 150 mOsm/kg","C. 50-60 mOsm/kg","D. 30 mOsm/kg"]','C','Without ADH, the collecting duct is impermeable to water. The tubular fluid leaving the TAL is already diluted to ~100 mOsm/kg (as NaCl is pumped out without water). Further modest NaCl reabsorption in the water-impermeable DCT dilutes fluid further to ~50-60 mOsm/kg minimum. This is the minimum achievable urine osmolality. Humans can excrete up to 10-12 L/day of dilute urine when ADH is absent and water intake is high.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','medium','mcq','Central diabetes insipidus is caused by insufficient ADH secretion. Which finding differentiates it from nephrogenic DI on a water deprivation test followed by desmopressin (DDAVP) administration?','["A. In central DI, urine remains dilute after DDAVP; in nephrogenic DI, urine concentrates after DDAVP","B. Serum osmolality rises in central DI but not nephrogenic DI after water deprivation","C. Central DI produces no urine at all; nephrogenic DI produces large amounts","D. In central DI, urine osmolality rises significantly after DDAVP (kidneys can respond); in nephrogenic DI, urine remains dilute despite DDAVP (kidneys cannot respond)"]','D','The water deprivation + DDAVP test: after deprivation, both types produce dilute urine (failure to concentrate). After DDAVP: central DI patients concentrate urine (>50% increase in osmolality) because their kidneys are intact and can respond to exogenous ADH. Nephrogenic DI patients fail to concentrate even with DDAVP because the defect is in the renal V2 receptor or downstream signalling. Partial forms are more complex.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','medium','mcq','SIADH (syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion) causes hyponatraemia. What is the mechanism of hyponatraemia in SIADH?','["A. Excess ADH causes inappropriate water retention in the collecting duct via AQP2, diluting plasma sodium; despite low plasma osmolality, ADH continues to be secreted; the kidney continues to excrete sodium (often concentrated) because volume is expanded and aldosterone is suppressed","B. ADH directly inhibits sodium reabsorption in the PCT","C. ADH increases aldosterone, causing sodium wasting","D. ADH stimulates thirst inappropriately, causing polydipsia and dilutional hyponatraemia"]','A','In SIADH, ADH is secreted despite normal or low plasma osmolality. AQP2 insertion continues, causing water retention that dilutes serum sodium. Volume expansion suppresses aldosterone and RAAS (aldosterone escape), and the ANP increases natriuresis. The key features are: hyponatraemia, low plasma osmolality, inappropriately high urine osmolality (>100 mOsm/kg, often >300), elevated urine Na+ (>40 mEq/L due to volume expansion), and normal renal, adrenal, and thyroid function.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','medium','mcq','Lithium, used for bipolar disorder, commonly causes nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. What is the mechanism?','["A. Lithium blocks V2 receptors directly","B. Lithium enters principal cells via ENaC and accumulates intracellularly, where it inhibits adenylyl cyclase and blocks the cAMP-PKA pathway, preventing AQP2 vesicle insertion into the apical membrane","C. Lithium destroys AQP2 genes permanently","D. Lithium causes inflammation of the collecting duct leading to fibrosis"]','B','Lithium enters collecting duct principal cells via ENaC (lithium has similar size to Na+). Intracellular lithium inhibits glycogen synthase kinase-3 (GSK-3) and adenylyl cyclase, blocking the V2-cAMP-PKA signalling cascade that normally triggers AQP2 phosphorylation and apical membrane insertion. Additionally, chronic lithium reduces AQP2 mRNA expression. The result is a nephrogenic DI-like state with polyuria and polydipsia. Amiloride (blocking ENaC) reduces lithium entry and partially treats this complication.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','medium','mcq','In a severely dehydrated patient, the urine/plasma osmolality ratio can reach approximately:','["A. 1:1 (isotonic urine)","B. 2:1","C. 4:1 (e.g., 1200/290 mOsm/kg)","D. 10:1"]','C','In maximal dehydration with maximal ADH secretion and an intact concentrating mechanism, urine osmolality can reach ~1200 mOsm/kg while plasma osmolality is ~300 mOsm/kg, giving a ratio of approximately 4:1. This is lower in elderly patients (who have reduced medullary gradient due to decreased urea and NaCl accumulation) and in patients with CKD, loop diuretic use, or protein malnutrition (less urea for recycling).','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','medium','mcq','An osmoreceptor neuron in the anterior hypothalamus detects a rise in plasma osmolality above the set point (~285 mOsm/kg). What is the order of physiological responses triggered?','["A. Thirst first, then ADH after a 30-minute delay","B. ADH is the only response; thirst plays no role","C. ANP is released to counteract the osmolality rise","D. ADH release from the posterior pituitary (occurs rapidly, within seconds to minutes) causes AQP2 insertion in the collecting duct; simultaneously thirst is stimulated; if intake occurs, osmolality normalises and both ADH and thirst are suppressed"]','D','Osmoreceptors (located near the anterior hypothalamus and subfornical organ) detect plasma osmolality changes as small as 1-2 mOsm/kg. ADH release begins within seconds of osmolality rising above threshold, causing rapid AQP2 insertion and water reabsorption. Thirst is also stimulated, promoting water intake. The combined response restores osmolality. The ADH response is proportional to osmolality -- a sigmoid-shaped dose-response curve describes the relationship.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','hard','mcq','A patient has plasma osmolality of 310 mOsm/kg and urine osmolality of 150 mOsm/kg despite being severely volume-depleted. What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the mechanism of failure?','["A. Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus -- the kidneys cannot respond to ADH (which is maximally secreted due to both osmotic and volume stimuli); V2 receptor or AQP2 dysfunction prevents water reabsorption in the collecting duct, causing large-volume dilute urine despite severe dehydration and very high plasma ADH levels","B. Central diabetes insipidus -- insufficient ADH secretion despite osmolality 310","C. Primary polydipsia -- excessive water intake diluting plasma and suppressing ADH","D. SIADH -- excess ADH causing dilute plasma and concentrated urine"]','A','The combination of high plasma osmolality (310 mOsm/kg), low urine osmolality (150 mOsm/kg despite volume depletion), and clinical dehydration strongly suggests nephrogenic DI. ADH will be maximally elevated (both osmotic stimulation at >295 mOsm/kg and volume depletion via baroreceptors strongly stimulate ADH), yet the kidney cannot respond. In central DI, DDAVP would restore concentrating ability. In this case, DDAVP would have no effect, confirming nephrogenic DI.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
INSERT INTO questions (professions, course, topic, category, subtopic, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, region, high_yield, year_level, source_reference) VALUES
('{"medicine"}','Medicine','Renal System','Anatomy & Physiology','Water Balance & Osmolality','hard','mcq','The relationship between plasma ADH concentration and urine osmolality is not linear. At very low ADH concentrations (near zero), what happens to urine osmolality, and what does this imply about the set point for ADH secretion?','["A. Urine osmolality rises to maximum even with trace ADH, implying extremely sensitive AQP2 channels","B. Urine osmolality is at minimum (~50-60 mOsm/kg) when ADH is absent or very low; even small increases in plasma ADH (1-2 pg/mL) cause sharp rises in urine osmolality; the osmotic threshold for ADH release (~285 mOsm/kg) is set just below normal plasma osmolality, ensuring the kidney operates near maximal diluting capacity at rest but can rapidly switch to concentration mode","C. Urine osmolality is 290 mOsm/kg at all ADH levels (osmosis equilibrates everything)","D. ADH has no effect below 5 pg/mL; above this a sudden jump to maximum occurs"]','B','The urine osmolality vs plasma ADH relationship is sigmoidal with a steep initial slope. At zero ADH, urine is maximally dilute (~50-60 mOsm/kg). As ADH rises from 0 to ~5 pg/mL, urine osmolality increases steeply to ~700 mOsm/kg. Further ADH increases yield diminishing returns (approaching the ~1200 mOsm/kg maximum). The osmotic threshold for ADH release is ~285 mOsm/kg -- just below normal osmolality. This means under normal conditions, mild fluctuations in water intake are buffered by small ADH adjustments; the system operates at high sensitivity near the threshold.','universal',true,'{year1,year2,year3,year4,year5,year6,practitioner}','Guyton & Hall Medical Physiology 14th Ed');
